<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">State of Quito</h1><p class="st-meta">1811 CE – 1812 CE · capital Quito</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An early patriot state in the Ecuadorian highlands that issued one of the first constitutions in Spanish America before royalist forces crushed it.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito's</a> first junta of 10 August 1809 was suppressed, and on 2 August 1810 royalist troops massacred imprisoned patriots in Quito. A second junta formed in September 1810, promoted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlos-montufar">Carlos Montúfar</a>, a royal commissioner who sided with the patriots; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-cuero-y-caicedo">José Cuero y Caicedo</a> later became its president. In December 1811 it broke with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-regency-of-spain-and-the-indies">Regency Council</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>In February 1812 a congress adopted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solemn-pact-of-society-and-union">Solemn Pact of Society and Union</a>, a constitution creating the State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a>, which still recognised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-vii-of-spain">Ferdinand VII</a> in principle while asserting self-rule. Bitter rivalry between the Montúfar faction and the Sánchez de Orellana family weakened the government, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuenca-ecuador">Cuenca</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guayaquil">Guayaquil</a> remained royalist.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Forces sent by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">viceroy</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toribio-montes">Toribio Montes</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a> in November 1812 and defeated the last patriots at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibarra">Ibarra</a> in December. The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/royal-audiencia-of-quito">Royal Audiencia of Quito</a> was restored until independence in 1822.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="republic, 1811 CE – 1812 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 38 L50 20 L80 38 Z"/><path d="M24 42 H76 M24 80 H76"/><path d="M30 44 V78 M43 44 V78 M57 44 V78 M70 44 V78"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">republic</p><p class="st-emblem-region">South America</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:95.74%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1811 CE – 1812 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Quito</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>José Cuero y Caicedo, Carlos Montúfar</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ecuador">Ecuador</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Free State of Quito, Estado de Quito</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1812&amp;focus=state-of-quito" aria-label="State of Quito on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2499 2186 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where State of Quito lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2819 2442L2817 2443M2819 2442L4910 989"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2817 2443h0M4910 989h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2819 2442h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2819 2442h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Quito. <a href="/atlas?year=1812&amp;focus=state-of-quito">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
